// A value-object invariant that calls `.matches(<regex>)` — the shape that makes
// each backend lift a REGULAR EXPRESSION into a second emitted file (the wire /
// request validator) beside the domain class that already had one.
//
// Minted by the fixture-shape audit (docs/audits/2026-09-29-fixture-shape-coverage.md).
// The corpus had NO regex invariant at all — zero of 89 fixtures — and only four
// models in the whole repo used `.matches(` anywhere (one elixir-vanilla-build
// fixture and three `examples/`).  So no compile gate on any backend ever saw a
// regex leave the domain emitter, and the evaluation's F-013 was exactly that:
// `Domain/ValueObjects/UnLocode.cs` emitted `using
// System.Text.RegularExpressions;` while
// `Application/…/Requests/…RequestValidators.cs` called `Regex.IsMatch` with no
// using at all, so `dotnet build` failed on ordinary modelling.
//
// The invariant is deliberately the PLAINEST possible: one `.matches` on one
// string field of one VO used by one aggregate.  The bug class is a missing
// IMPORT in a second file, so nothing more elaborate reaches it, and anything
// more elaborate would blur which emitter is under test.
//
// Per-backend state at mint time:
//
//   dotnet  FIXED — F-013's own subject.  `CodeRequestValidators.cs` now carries
//           `using System.Text.RegularExpressions;` alongside the domain class's.
//           Verified with `dotnet build /warnaserror` on sdk:10.0.
//   node    free — the regex is emitted as an inline literal
//           (`/^[A-Z]{5}$/.test(...)`), so there is no import to forget.
//   java    correct — `CodeValidator.java` imports `java.util.regex.Pattern` and
//           hoists `Pattern.compile(...)` to a static field.
//   elixir  free — `Regex`/`=~` are in Elixir's Kernel, nothing to import.
//   python  FIXED (M-T9.84).  `app/http/wire_models.py` used to emit
//           `re.search("^[A-Z]{5}$", self.value)` with no `import re` (ruff
//           F821) while the domain half imported it — F-013's defect class, on a
//           second backend, found the moment this shape existed.  The renderer
//           now writes `re` through a `ref()` marker, so every module that
//           spells it derives its own import; python is back in `backends:`.
//
// COMPILE-TIER ONLY — no runtime block, same reason as `vo-cross-context.ddd`:
// a recorded behavioural case must ship a reviewed wire golden
// (`golden-coverage.test.ts`), capturable only from a booted stack on every
// backend, and python's wire model does not import `re` (above), so its boot
// fails.  The wire-boundary rejection this shape most wants asserted — 422 on a
// bad value, not 500 and not silently stored — is exactly what a runtime block
// should carry, so add it, with its golden, in the same PR that fixes python.
system VoRegexInvariant {
  subdomain Registry {
    context Codes {
      valueobject Code {
        value: string
        invariant value.matches("^[A-Z]{5}$") message "must be five uppercase letters"
        derived label: string = value
      }

      aggregate Berth with crudish {
        name: string
        // The VO crosses the wire, so its invariant is lifted into the request
        // validator as well as the domain class — the two emitters whose import
        // lists must agree.
        code: Code
        derived display: string = name
      }

      repository Berths for Berth { }
    }
  }

  api CodesApi from Registry
  storage primary { type: postgres }
  resource codesState { for: Codes, kind: state, use: primary }

  deployable d {
    platform: __PLATFORM__
    contexts: [Codes]
    dataSources: [codesState]
    serves: CodesApi
    port: 4000
  }
}
